  "from": "thingalog-strategist",
  "to": "catdef-strategist",
  "subject": "Cross-spec: ratify the DENORMALIZATION pattern + cross-catalog value-sharing semantic as canonical catdef substrate behavior. Context: Thingalog ratified namespace-URL-model proposal 2026-06-09 (per-user namespace + subcats-as-root-siblings + publish-as-premium); driven by AI-as-peer thesis (frictionless catalog creation). Cross-catalog value sharing becomes first-class use case — one shared Country subcat usable from Boatbuilder + Boat Owners + Naval Architect. What makes it safe = the denormalization pattern (values cached in referencing catalog: id + label + visible_attributes; rendering filters/chips/breadcrumbs/drills w/o source-catalog round-trip; subcat-catalog access only needed to BROWSE source). Enables 3 load-bearing capabilities: (1) cross-catalog reuse w/o cascading access controls; (2) subcat independence (rename/move/private/delete doesn't break parents); (3) free-floating subcats as root-level catalogs (the namespace model). ASK: ratify as canonical catdef substrate behavior. Specifically: (a) codify denormalization invariant — referencing catalog stores cached state (id/label/visible_attributes) sufficient to render w/o source round-trip; (b) codify eventual-consistency semantic — label changes propagate over time, substrates SHOULD provide refresh path; (c) codify cross-catalog visibility — referencing a value does NOT grant parent readers access to source catalog; subcat-catalog access is separate authz decision. Two related substrate-shape flags (not asks): (1) per-user-namespace routing convention worth documenting as recommended pattern (not requirement) for other Type-2 catdef implementations; (2) cross-catalog field targeting — Thingalog's list_subcats is per-catalog today (graph.py:4029); new model needs list_namespace_subcats equivalent; catdef may want to think about whether substrate spec should document cross-catalog field targeting (field 'target' is typically same-catalog today). No urgency; Thingalog implementation proceeds through phases regardless of catdef cycle. Standard catdef cycle.",
  "sent": "2026-06-09T17:30:00-04:00",
  "action_required": false,
  "body": "Thingalog ratified a foundational URL-model proposal 2026-06-09 (per-user namespace + subcats-as-root-level-siblings + publish-to-global-subdomain as premium escape; full proposal s:/projects/thingalog/proposals/namespace-url-model.md). Driven by AI-as-peer thesis — frictionless catalog creation requires per-user namespaces so creation costs nothing + collides w/ no one. Consequence: cross-catalog value sharing becomes first-class — a Country subcat is one shared root-level catalog within owner's namespace, used by Boatbuilder + Boat Owners + Naval Architect etc., not duplicated per-parent-catalog. What makes it safe is the DENORMALIZATION pattern Thingalog has used in production but never canonicalized at catdef spec level. THE PATTERN: when a catalog references values from another catalog (subcat used as value source), values are CACHED at the referencing catalog level. Referencing catalog stores enough denormalized data about each referenced value (label, key visible attributes, value's UUID) to render filters / chips / breadcrumbs / lens drills WITHOUT requiring access to source subcat. Concrete: reader of mountainman23/dishwashers filtering by Brand=Bosch sees Bosch rendered w/ no round-trip to Brand subcat; Brand subcat may be public/private/absent to reader; parent catalog renders correctly regardless; only when reader NAVIGATES TO BROWSE Brand subcat itself is source access required. 3 LOAD-BEARING CAPABILITIES this enables: (1) cross-catalog reuse w/o cascading access controls (shared Country across many parents doesn't expose Country catalog owner to every parent's readers); (2) subcat independence (rename/move/private/delete doesn't break parents — they continue to render cached label); (3) free-floating subcats as root-level catalogs (Thingalog namespace model), not subordinate parent/child URL structures. THE ASK — ratify as canonical catdef substrate behavior, specifically: (1) CODIFY DENORMALIZATION INVARIANT — when catalog references value from another catalog via Enumerated field, referencing catalog stores enough cached state (minimum: value's id UUID + label + possibly visible_attributes) to render filters / chips / breadcrumbs / drills WITHOUT round-tripping to source catalog. (2) CODIFY EVENTUAL-CONSISTENCY SEMANTIC — when referenced value's label changes in its source catalog, cached label in referencing catalogs may take time to propagate OR may not propagate at all (depending on implementation); substrates SHOULD provide refresh path; substrates MAY surface staleness to AI peers / Karens. (3) CODIFY CROSS-CATALOG VISIBILITY SEMANTIC — referencing a value does NOT grant parent catalog's readers access to source catalog itself; subcat-catalog access is separate authorization decision (reader follows link to BROWSE source catalog). These 3 commitments make cross-catalog reuse safe + portable across catdef implementations. Today Thingalog-implementation-emergent; want substrate-canonical. TWO RELATED SUBSTRATE-SHAPE FLAGS (not asks for this cycle — awareness for catdef longer-arc thinking): (1) Thingalog's new URL model <handle>.thingalog.app/<slug> is Thingalog-application routing decision not catdef substrate decision (catdef doesn't dictate routing shape) — but other catdef implementations (especially future Type-2 ones) may face same friction-vs-collision tradeoff; catdef may want to note per-user-namespace convention as RECOMMENDED PATTERN not requirement. (2) Today Thingalog's list_subcats is per-catalog-scoped (graph.py:4029); new model needs list_namespace_subcats equivalent so Enumerated field can target cross-namespace subcats; catdef may want to think about whether substrate spec should document cross-catalog field targeting (today field 'target' is typically same-catalog). RATIFICATION PATH: standard catdef cycle. Thingalog namespace-URL-model proposal proceeds through phases regardless of catdef cycle timing — denormalization pattern in production today, this memo asks catdef to recognize as canonical so portable to other catdef substrate implementations. NO URGENCY from Thingalog side.",
